# GraphQL Patterns

The client-facing API is GraphQL-only. **No REST controllers, no Swagger for anything your own frontend/mobile clients call.** WHEN the caller is a machine that cannot speak your GraphQL contract (identity-provider trigger, webhook callback), a narrow secured REST controller is correct — the signal is WHO calls it; see [architecture.md](./architecture.md#api-style).

## Code-first approach

Combine `@ObjectType()` and `@Entity()` on the same class:

```ts
@ObjectType()
@Entity({ tableName: 'users' })
export class User extends BaseEntity {
  @Field()
  @Property({ unique: true })
  email!: string;

  @Field(() => UserRole)
  @Enum({ items: () => UserRole })
  role: UserRole & Opt = UserRole.USER;

  // Computed field (GraphQL only, not persisted)
  @Field()
  get fullName(): string {
    return `${this.firstName} ${this.lastName}`.trim();
  }
}
```

## Input types

```ts
@InputType()
export class RegisterUserInput {
  @Field()
  @IsEmail()
  email!: string;

  @Field()
  @MinLength(8)
  password!: string;

  @Field()
  @IsNotEmpty()
  firstName!: string;

  @Field({ nullable: true })
  lastName?: string;
}
```

### Two validation layers, different jobs

- **class-validator on `@InputType` = transport SHAPE** — format, length, presence (`@IsEmail`, `@MinLength`).
- **Entity invariants = BUSINESS rules** — uniqueness-bearing normalization, cross-field rules; they live in the entity factory (see [entities.md](./entities.md)).
- Don't duplicate one as the other: shape goes on the DTO, meaning goes on the entity.

**Foot-gun (THIS repo): the global `ValidationPipe` runs with `whitelist` + `forbidNonWhitelisted`** (`src/main.ts` and `src/lambda.ts`) — an input field with `@Field` but NO class-validator decorator is INVISIBLE to validation: with `forbidNonWhitelisted` the request is REJECTED ("property X should not exist"); with `whitelist` alone it would be silently stripped. Either way the field never works — every input field needs at least one validator (or `@Allow`).

One validation library per layer; don't introduce a second for one DTO — e.g. (THIS repo): FE/mobile forms use RHF + Zod, the backend is class-validator-only.

## Response types

```ts
@ObjectType()
export class RegistrationResponse {
  @Field()
  success!: boolean;

  @Field({ nullable: true })
  message?: string;

  @Field({ nullable: true })
  userId?: string;

  static success(data: Partial<RegistrationResponse>): RegistrationResponse {
    return Object.assign(new RegistrationResponse(), { success: true, ...data });
  }
}
```

## Resolver pattern

```ts
@Resolver(() => User)
export class UserResolver {
  constructor(private readonly userService: UserService) {}

  @Query(() => User)
  @UseGuards(AuthGuard)
  async me(@CurrentUser() user: CurrentUserPayload): Promise<User> {
    return this.userService.findById(user.userId); // normalized payload — see auth.md
  }

  @Mutation(() => User)
  @UseGuards(AuthGuard, RolesGuard)
  @Roles(UserRole.ADMIN)
  async updateUser(@Args('input') input: UpdateUserInput): Promise<User> {
    // ...
  }
}
```

## Avoid N+1 in field resolvers (required)

A `@ResolveField` that queries per parent object fires one query **per row** in a list —
the classic GraphQL N+1. Batch these with a **request-scoped DataLoader**.

```typescript
// ❌ BANNED - runs once per user in the list (N+1)
@ResolveField(() => [Task])
async assignedTasks(@Parent() user: User): Promise<Task[]> {
  return this.em.find(Task, { assignee: user.id }); // one query per parent
}

// ✅ DataLoader batches all parent ids into a single `WHERE assignee_id IN (...)`
@ResolveField(() => [Task])
async assignedTasks(@Parent() user: User): Promise<Task[]> {
  return this.tasksByUserLoader.load(user.id);
}
```

- One DataLoader per relation, created **per request** (never shared across requests — it caches).
- The batch function must issue a **single** `{ <fk>: { $in: keys } }` query and map results back to keys.
- Alternatively, `populate` the relation in the root query when it's always requested.

Full query-performance gates (pagination, projection, indexes, batching) live in
`.cursor/rules/sql-performance.mdc`.

## Rules

- One file = one resolver class
- Resolvers don't contain business logic — delegate to feature services
- A read that needs **authorization or a computed/cross-aggregate value is NOT a dumb passthrough**: apply the guard at the resolver (`@UseGuards`) and keep any computation/cross-aggregate fetch in the feature or domain layer — the resolver stays logic-free, but the query is a *named* read, not a generic parameterized one (see [domain-services.md](./domain-services.md) read-shape). Trivial CRUD with no such logic should NOT be dressed up in extra machinery either — match the shape to the read.
- **The FE/mobile GraphQL selection set is the contract.** If you change a query's arguments or return shape, the consuming FE/mobile documents and their codegen MUST change in the same diff (code-review-verifier enforces this coherence check). Don't make a query selection-driven on the backend and leave consumers to drift.
- Errors return as GraphQL errors with `extensions.code` — see [exceptions.md](./exceptions.md)
- HTTP status codes don't apply — return error codes via `extensions`
- Subscriptions are **not enabled** — they're opt-in in `@nestjs/graphql` (no `subscriptions` config = off), and Lambda can't hold the connections anyway — see [lambda.md](./lambda.md)
